t to the river and see what the opponent has. It is likely my opponents have small pairs or high cards.\n\nTherefore, the action is Check (0).\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You hold a pair of Queens (Second Pair) with a 9 kicker. The board contains an Ace and three diamonds, presenting significant flush and straight possibilities. The betting history is extremely passive, with checks on all previous streets. The opponent's range is likely weak, but could include a random Ace or a slow-played trap.\n\nReason: Since you have a hand with showdown value (Second Pair) but vulnerable to an Ace or a Flush, and the pot is small, there is little value in betting. Betting forces worse hands to fold and better hands to call or raise. Checking back allows you to realize your equity without risking more chips, securing the win against weaker hands like a pair of 10s, 8s, or high cards.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
